<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1831 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A patriotic song of 1831, also called &quot;America&quot;, sung to the tune of Britain's &quot;God Save the King&quot;.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Samuel Francis Smith</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the melody of &quot;God Save the King&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>American</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Andover, Massachusetts, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written and first sung 1831; published 1832</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Samuel Francis Smith, written 1831, first published 1832)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a>&quot;, also called &quot;America&quot;, was written in 1831 by Samuel Francis Smith while he was a student at the Andover Theological Seminary in Andover, Massachusetts. The composer Lowell Mason had asked him to translate, or write new words for, a collection of German songs, one of which was set to the melody of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;, the de facto national anthem of the United Kingdom. Smith gave Mason the words he had written to that tune. They were first sung in public on 4 July 1831, at a children's Independence Day celebration at Park Street Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, and first printed in 1832. Setting new words to the British royal anthem was a contrafactum: the tune that stood for the British monarchy was made to speak for American democracy.</p><p>Through the 19th century and the early 20th the song was one of the de facto national anthems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, along with songs such as &quot;Hail, Columbia&quot;, until &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a>&quot; was adopted as the official national anthem in 1931. Other words were set to it as well: an abolitionist version by A. G. Duncan in 1843 named both white and black people, and a further verse was added to the original for Washington's centennial celebration. Charles Ives wrote organ variations on it, Variations on &quot;America&quot;.</p><p>Some of its best-known performances belong to the struggle for civil rights. On Easter Sunday, 9 April 1939, the African American contralto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marian-anderson">Marian Anderson</a> sang it at the Lincoln Memorial: she had been barred from singing at the DAR Constitution Hall under its whites-only policy, and after a national outcry, and with the support of Eleanor Roosevelt, the concert was held on the memorial's steps before more than 75,000 people and a radio audience of millions. On 28 August 1963 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> recited its first verse near the end of his &quot;I Have a Dream&quot; speech at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a> for Jobs and Freedom. Aretha Franklin sang it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barack-obama">Barack Obama</a>'s first inauguration on 20 January 2009, and Kelly Clarkson at his second.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic hymn of love for the singer's country. Set to the British royal anthem, it turns a song for a king into a song for a free people and its liberty; its mood is solemn and devoted.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>MY country, 't is of thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sweet land of liberty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of thee I sing;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Land where my fathers died,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Land of the pilgrims' pride,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From every mountain side</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let freedom ring.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>My native country, thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Land of the noble free,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thy name I love;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I love thy rocks and rills,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Thy woods and templed hills;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>My heart with rapture thrills</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Like that above.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let music swell the breeze,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And ring from all the trees</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sweet Freedom's song;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let mortal tongues awake,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let all that breathe partake,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let rocks their silence break,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The sound prolong.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our fathers' God, to Thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Author of liberty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To Thee we sing;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Long may our land be bright</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With Freedom's holy light;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Protect us by Thy might,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Great God, our King.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Samuel Francis Smith, Poems of Home and Country (1895). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Poems_of_Home_and_Country%2FAmerica" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="eWJI0oA7fLM" data-title="My Country, 'Tis of Thee: The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eWJI0oA7fLM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">abolitionist version (A. G. Duncan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1843</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words naming white and black people</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations on &quot;America&quot; (Charles Ives)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">organ variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">I Have a Dream (Martin Luther King Jr.)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first verse recited in the speech</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li></ul></section>
